### Tested versions

- Reproducible on latest .NET Godot version 4.4.1

### System information

Godot v4.4.1.stable.mono - Windows 11 (build 26100) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.7640) - AMD Ryzen 5 5600X 6-Core Processor (12 threads)

### Issue description

When using the CollisionResult array from ShapeCast2D it causes memory usage to spike when theres things colliding. 

You can ofcourse force a GC.Collect() call but using other Godot.Collections.Array types does not cause this behavior to happen. Alternatively the same script in GDScript does not have this behavior happen. 



### Steps to reproduce

From a blank project create a Test Scene, With a Node2D, A ShapeCast2D child and multiple CharacterBody2D children as well. 

In ShapeCast2D attach the script:

```cs
using Godot;
using System;

public partial class ShapeCast2d : ShapeCast2D
{
    public override void _PhysicsProcess(double delta)
    {
        if (IsColliding())
        {
            Variant col = CollisionResult;
        }
    }

}

```

Check static mem usage

Calling GC.Collect() to force a collection will stop this but to me this seems more like a bandaid fix since this behaviour does not happen with GDScript or with other types of Godot.Collection.Array. Calling Dispose on the variable and CollisionResult also does not fix the issue - it still climbs.

GDScript which works as expected:

```gdscript
extends ShapeCast2D

func _physics_process(delta: float) -> void:
    if is_colliding():
	var col: Variant = collision_result[0]
```

